\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Comparison between \ac {PPS}- and \ac {PNPS}-based reductions of the subnetwork $(A,C)$. Subfigures (\subref  {fig:pps_before})--(\subref  {fig:pps_after}) and (\subref  {fig:pnps_before_1})--(\subref  {fig:pnps_after_1}) show the \emph  {same} graph; only the counting scheme used for the edge \aclp {NL} differs (\acp {PPS} on the top pair, \acp {PNPS} on the middle pair). Red marks the elements affected by the reduction: in the \ac {PPS} case (\subref  {fig:pps_after}), both the replacement edge and the \aclp {NL} of all remaining edges change, whereas in the \ac {PNPS} cases (\subref  {fig:pnps_after_1}) and (\subref  {fig:pnps_after_2}) only the replacement edge is affected and all other \aclp {NL} are preserved. Dash-dotted edges in (\subref  {fig:pnps_before_2})--(\subref  {fig:pnps_after_2}) depict the additional path via $H$ that turns $(A,G)$ into a \ac {PNPS}.}}{65}{figure.caption.112}\protected@file@percent }
